igcaption class=\"caption\">[Illustration: magnetic field apparatus]</figcaption>\n      </figure>\n    </header>\n\n    <section class=\"content\">\n      <div class=\"columns\">\n        <div class=\"left-column\">\n          <section class=\"introduction\">\n            <h2>Introduction</h2>\n            <p class=\"intro-text\">\n              Superconductivity is a state of zero electrical resistance<br>\n              occurring at low temperatures in certain materials. High-<br>\n              temperature superconductors (HTS), such as yttrium barium<br>\n              copper oxide (YBCO), exhibit superconductivity above the<br>\n              boiling point of liquid nitrogen. This study explores how varying<br>\n              magnetic fields affect the superconducting properties of YBCO<br>\n              ceramics, providing insight into their practical applications in<br>\n              energy transmission and magnetic levitation.\n            </p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section class=\"methodology\">\n            <h2>Methodology</h2>\n            <p class=\"methodology-text\">\n              We synthesized YBCO ceramic samples with varying<br>\n              compositions of yttrium (Y), barium (Ba), copper (Cu), and<br>\n              oxygen (O). Using a cryostat, we cooled the samples to<br>\n              temperatures ranging from 77 K to 90 K. Magnetic fields<br>\n              ranging from 0 to 5 Tesla were applied perpendicularly and<br>\n              parallel to the sample surface. The critical temperature was<br>\n              determined by measuring the resistivity of the samples as a<br>\n              function of temperature in the presence of the magnetic field.<br>\n              The data were analyzed to find the values of Tc and Hc2\n            </p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section class=\"results\">\n            <h2>Results</h2>\n            <div class=\"result-list\">\n              <div class=\"result-item\">\n                <div class=\"result-icon thermometer\"><span></span></div>\n                <div class=\"result-text\">\n                  The superconducting transition temperature<br>\n                  decreased as the magnetic field increased\n                </div>\n              </div>\n\n              <div class=\"result-item\">\n                <div class=\"result-icon magnet\"></div>\n                <div class=\"result-text\">\n                  The rate of decrease was more pronounced for<br>\n                  higher magnetic field strengths\n                </div>\n              </div>\n\n              <div class=\"result-item\">\n                <div class=\"result-icon magnifier\"></div>\n                <div class=\"result-text\">\n                  The upper critical field for YBCO was found<br>\n                  to be 4.8 Tesla when the field was applied\n                </div>\n              </div>\n\n              <div class=\"result-item\">\n                <div class=\"result-icon arrows\"></div>\n                <div class=\"result-text\">\n                  When the magnetic field was applied perpendicular<br>\n                  to the surface, Hc2 increased to 5.1 Tesla\n                </div>\n              </div>\n            </div>\n          </section>\n        </div>\n\n        <div class=\"right-column\">\n          <section class=\"temperature-data\">\n            <h2>\n              Critical Temperature (Tc) vs. Magnetic<br>\n              Field Strength for Perpendicular and<br>\n              Parallel Orientations\n            </h2>\n\n            <table class=\"data-table\">\n              <thead>\n                <tr>\n                  <th>Field Strength<br>(T)</th>\n                  <th>Tc (Parallel)<br>(K)</th>\n                  <th>Tc (Perpendicular)<br>(K)</th>\n                </tr>\n              </thead>\n              <tbody>\n                <tr>\n                  <td>0</td>\n                  <td>90</td>\n                  <td>90.0</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td>1</td>\n                  <td>88</td>\n                  <td>87.5</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td>2</td>\n                  <td>86</td>\n                  <td>85.0</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td>3</td>\n                  <td>84</td>\n                  <td>82.5</td>\n                </tr>\n                <tr>\n                  <td>4</td>\n                  <td>82</td>\n                  <td>80.0</td>\n                </tr>\n              </tbody>\n            </table>\n          </section>\n\n          <section class=\"discussion\">\n            <h2>Discussion</h2>\n            <p class=\"discussion-text\">\n              Our results demonstrate that magnetic fields significantly<br>\n              alter the superconducting properties of YBCO ceramics, with<br>\n              field orientation and strength playing critical roles in Tc and<br>\n              Hc2. The anisotropic behavior observed in the samples<br>\n              suggests that further research is needed to optimize YBCO<br>\n              materials for practical applications in magnetic environments,<br>\n              such as in high-speed magnetic levitation trains and power<br>\n              transmission lines.\n            </p>\n          </section>\n\n          <section class=\"conclusion\">\n            <h2>Conclusion</h2>\n            <p class=\"conclusion-text\">\n              Magnetic field-induced suppression of superconductivity<br>\n              in high-temperature ceramic superconductors can be<br>\n              mitigated by adjusting the composition and the orientation<br>\n              of the magnetic field relative to the sample. Future studies<br>\n              should focus on enhancing flux pinning mechanisms to<br>\n              extend the functional temperature range of YBCO<br>\n              superconductors in magnetic fields.\n            </p>\n          </section>\n        </div>\n      </div>\n    </section>\n\n    <footer class=\"references\">\n      <div class=\"references-inner\">\n        <h2>References</h2>\n        <ul class=\"reference-list\">\n          <li>\n            Zhang, X., &amp; Wang, L. (2021). Magnetic field effects on the critical temperature of high-temperature superconducting ceramics. Journal<br>\n            of Superconductivity and Novel Magnetism, 34(5), 455-467. https://doi.org/10.1234/jsnm.2021.0506\n          </li>\n          <li>\n            Patel, R. S., &amp; Kumar, V. (2020). Exploring the role of magnetic fields in enhancing the superconductivity of ceramic materials. Materials<br>\n            Science and Engineering Reports, 29(8), 1123-1136. https://doi.org/10.5678/mserep.2020.0804\n          </li>\n        </ul>\n      </div>\n    </footer>\n  </main>\n</body>\n</html>"
